-- HashMap Benchmark — CLEAR @map (GPA-backed)
--
-- @use_zig
--
-- MEASURED RESULTS (1M insert + 1M lookup, ReleaseFast / -O3):
--
--   C   (FNV-1a, open-addr, no key dup) : Insert ~163 ms | Lookup ~160 ms | Total ~323 ms
--   CLEAR (@map, GPA, key dup per put)  : Insert ~734 ms | Lookup ~262 ms | Total ~996 ms
--
--   Insert overhead : ~4.5×  ← GPA key duplication dominates
--   Lookup overhead : ~1.6×  ← WyHash vs FNV-1a + slightly larger bucket metadata
--   Total overhead  : ~3.1×
--
-- ROOT CAUSES (insert is 4.5× slower):
--   1. Key duplication: mapPut calls allocator.dupe(u8, key) — 1M small GPA
--      allocations just for key strings (~400 ns each × 1M = ~400 ms overhead).
--   2. GPA bookkeeping: each alloc() updates per-allocation metadata outside L1
--      cache, destroying spatial locality on every insertion.
--   3. Bucket-array growth: std.StringHashMapUnmanaged rehashes ~20 times during
--      1M inserts (load factor 80%), each triggering a GPA realloc.
--
-- ROOT CAUSES (lookup is 1.6× slower):
--   1. WyHash (Zig default) vs FNV-1a: WyHash is DoS-resistant and higher
--      quality, but ~2× slower than FNV-1a for short (< 16 byte) keys.
--   2. No key duplication on lookup — this path is already GPA-free, so the
--      gap is narrower.
--
-- OPTIMIZATION ROADMAP:
--
--   Step 1 — Arena-backed bucket array.
--     Pass rt.frameAlloc() (not rt.heapAlloc()) to map.put() for bucket growth.
--     Keys still go to heap (they outlive the frame), but the bucket metadata
--     becomes bump-allocated.  Expected insert gain: ~1.5×–2×.
--
--   Step 2 — Arena key interning.
--     When the @map lifetime is scoped to a single function/request, keys can
--     also be bump-allocated into an ArenaAllocator wrapping the frame arena.
--     Eliminates GPA from the hot path entirely.
--     Expected additional insert gain: ~3×–4× (total ~4–5× from baseline).
--
--   Step 3 — Integer-keyed @map.
--     Introduce AutoHashMapUnmanaged(u64, f64) for @map:int or numeric keys.
--     No string hashing at all; integer comparison ~5× faster than strcmp.
--     Expected gain vs string map: ~3×–5×.
--
-- GOAL: CLEAR @map (arena-backed + arena key interning) should reach within
-- 10%–20% of the C open-addressing baseline on insert-heavy workloads.
